 one \\(\\ell_{i}\\);\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny*, i.e. are not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. The sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_{t}^{\\;(\\mathrm{sunny})}\\;:\\; y = x + t .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_{t}^{\\;(\\mathrm{sunny})}\\) is \\(1\\); hence it is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions, so each \\(\\ell_{t}^{\\;(\\mathrm{sunny})}\\) is sunny.  \nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(\\ell_{0}^{\\;(\\mathrm{sunny})},\\dots ,\\ell_{k-1}^{\\;(\\mathrm{sunny})}\\) are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\nA lattice point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_{t}^{\\;(\\mathrm{sunny})}\\) exactly when  \n\n\\[\nb-a = t .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the set  \n\n\\[\nS_{k}:=\\bigcup_{t=0}^{k-1}\\ell_{t}^{\\;(\\mathrm{sunny})}\n\\]\n\ncontains **all** points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\), \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. The remaining points\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{k}:=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1,\\; b-a\\ge k\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{k}\\) then \\(b\\ge a+k\\); in particular \\(b\\ge k+1\\).\nWrite \\(b = j\\) with \\(j\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nFrom the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na \\le n+1-j .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(b-a\\ge k\\) we also have  \n\n\\[\na \\le b-k = j-k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence for a fixed \\(j\\) the admissible values of \\(a\\) are the integers  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le \\min\\bigl\\{\\,n+1-j,\\;j-k\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nA direct inspection of the two expressions shows that for every \\(j\\) with\n\\(k+1\\le j\\le n\\) the minimum equals \\(j-k\\).  Indeed,\n\\(j-k\\le n+1-j\\) is equivalent to \\(2j\\le n+1+k\\), which holds because\n\\(j\\le n\\) and \\(k