  \n\n\\[\nT_{k}:=\\bigl\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1,\\; b-a\\ge k\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_{k}\\) then \\(b\\ge a+k\\ge k+1\\); write \\(b=j\\) with \\(j\\in\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n+1-j\\), while \\(b-a\\ge k\\) gives \\(a\\le j-k\\).  \nSince \\(j\\le n\\) and \\(k\\ge 0\\), one checks that \\(j-k\\le n+1-j\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le j-k .\n\\]\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{T_{k}= \\bigcup_{j=k+1}^{n}\\;\\{(a,j)\\mid 1\\le a\\le j-k\\}} . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_{k}\\) lies on the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{j}:\\; y=j\\;},\\qquad j=k+1,\\dots ,n . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so none of them is sunny.\n\n#### 1.3 The whole family\nCollect the lines defined in (1) and (4):\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L\n   =\\bigl\\{\\ell^{\\text{sun}}_{t}\\mid 0\\le t\\le k-1\\bigr\\}\n    \\cup\n    \\bigl\\{\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{j}\\mid k+1\\le j\\le n\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nThe two sub\u2011collections are disjoint, hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L| = k + (n-k)=n .\n\\]\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the first \\(k\\) lines have slope \\(1\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are horizontal.  Therefore \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage of required points.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\) be a lattice point with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n* If \\(b-a\\le k-1\\) then, by (2), \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell^{\\text{sun}}_{\\,b-a}\\in\\mathcal L\\).\n* If \\(b-a\\ge k\\) then \\((a,b)\\in T_{k}\\) and by (3) it lies on \\(\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{b}\\in\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus every required point belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Verification of the extreme values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The construction yields only the horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\); none is sunny, and they cover all points because the condition \\(b-a\\ge 0\\) holds for every point in the triangle.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  The construction yields the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(y=x, y=x+1,\\dots ,y=x+n-1\\); there are no horizontal lines, and every point satisfies \\(b-a\\le n-1\\), so it lies on one of these lines.\n\nBoth extreme cases satisfy the problem\u2019s requirements, confirming that the construction works for every \\(k\\) in the whole interval.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
