 line that contains **all** points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on the removed line and that does not coincide with any line already present.  \n   Because any two distinct points determine a unique line, we can always select such a sunny line: we take the two extreme points of the removed line (with respect to the natural ordering on the lattice) and draw the line through them; this line cannot be parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\) (the extreme points have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, different \\(y\\)-coordinates and their sum is not constant), hence it is sunny.  \n\n   Performing this replacement for exactly \\(n-k\\) lines yields a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing precisely \\(k\\) non\u2011sunny (i.e. sunny) lines and still covering every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe two steps together give the desired construction for any \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 1 \u2013 A covering by non\u2011sunny lines\n\nDefine the following set of lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathcal H &=\\{\\,y=j\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\,\\},\\\\[2mm]\n\\mathcal V &=\\{\\,x=1\\,\\},\\\\[2mm]\n\\mathcal D &=\\{\\,x+y=n+1\\,\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal H\\) are horizontal, the line in \\(\\mathcal V\\) is vertical, and the line in \\(\\mathcal D\\) is parallel to the diagonal \\(x+y=0\\); hence none of them is sunny.  \nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_0:=\\mathcal H\\cup\\mathcal V\\cup\\mathcal D .\n\\]\n\nWe have \\(|\\mathcal L_0|=(n-1)+1+1=n\\).\n\n**Claim.** Every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\). Because \\(a\\ge 1\\) and \\(b\\ge 1\\) we have two possibilities.\n\n1. If \\(a=1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\in\\mathcal V\\).\n\n2. If \\(a\\ge 2\\) then \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n-1\\). Hence \\(b\\) is an integer between \\(1\\) and \\(n-1\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=b\\in\\mathcal H\\).\n\nFinally, if \\(a+b=n+1\\) the point also lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\in\\mathcal D\\). \u220e\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) is a family of \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Step 2 \u2013 Introducing sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall replace exactly \\(n-k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) by sunny lines, leaving the remaining \\(k\\) lines unchanged.  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal R\\subseteq\\mathcal L_0\\) be any subset of size \\(n-k\\).  \nFor each line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal R\\) consider the set  \n\n\\[\nP_\\ell:=\\{\\,P\\in S_n\\mid P\\text{ lies on }\\ell\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(\\ell\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L_0\\), the set \\(P_\\ell\\) is non\u2011empty.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nP_\\ell^{\\min }=(a_{\\min },b_{\\min }),\\qquad \nP_\\ell^{\\max }=(a_{\\max },b_{\\max })\n\\]\n\nbe the points of \\(P_\\ell\\) with minimal and maximal first coordinate (equivalently, the two extreme points of \\(P_\\ell\\) along the line).  \nDefine \\(\\ell'\\) to be the (unique) line passing through \\(P_\\ell^{\\min }\\) and \\(P_\\ell^{\\max }\\).\n\n**Lemma 2.** The line \\(\\ell'\\) is sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince the two extreme points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, \\(\\ell'\\) is not vertical.  \nTheir \\(y\\)-coordinates are also distinct (otherwise the two points would coincide), so \\(\\ell'\\) is not horizontal.  \nFinally, the sums \\(a_{\\min }+b_{\\min }\\) and \\(a_{\\max }+b_{\\max }\\) are different because the points lie on the same line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) which is either horizontal, vertical, or the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\); in each of these three cases the sum \\(x+y\\) is not constant along the line except for the diagonal, but for the diagonal the two extreme points satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\) for both, which would make them identical \u2013 a contradiction. Hence the sum is not constant, so \\(\\ell'\\) is not parallel to \\(x+y=0\\). \u220e\n\nThus each \\(\\ell'\\) is a sunny line that contains **all** points of \\(P_\\ell\\).  \n\nNow replace every \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal R\\) by its corresponding \\(\\ell'\\).  \nDenote the resulting family of lines by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L:=\\bigl(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\mathcal R\\bigr)\\cup\\{\\ell'\\mid \\ell\\in\\mathcal R\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the replacement lines are distinct (each \\(\\ell'\\) contains a point of \\(S_n\\) that does not belong to any other line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\mathcal R\\)), the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  \n\nBy construction, every point of \\(S_n\\) that was covered by a line of \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) remains covered: if the covering line was left unchanged, the point is still on that line; if the covering line was replaced, the point belongs to the corresponding \\(\\ell'\\). Consequently \\(\\mathcal L\\) still covers all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThe number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) equals \\(|\\mathcal R|=n-k\\) (the replaced lines) plus the number of sunny lines already present in \\(\\mathcal L_0\\setminus\\mathcal R\\). Since \\(\\mathcal L_0\\) contains **no** sunny lines, the total number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L\\) is exactly \\(|\\mathcal R|=n-k\\).  \n\nHence the configuration \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) non\u2011sunny lines, i.e. exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions, with exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny. Therefore all such \\(k\\) are admissible. \u220e"
