ny.  \n   Using the bounds from step\u202f2 we obtain a universal upper bound\n   \\[\n   |T_{n}|\\;=\\;\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\;\\le\\;(n-1)\\cdot\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   The right\u2011hand side is derived by observing that the maximal possible\n   contribution of any non\u2011sunny line is \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) points (the diagonal\n   family attains this maximum when \\(c=\\lfloor (n+2)/2\\rfloor\\)).  \n\n4. **Deriving a contradiction.**  \n   For every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) one checks that\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2} > (n-1)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n   Indeed,\n   \\[\n   \\frac{n(n+1)}{2}-(n-1)\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   =\\begin{cases}\n      \\dfrac{n+2}{2}>0, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[4pt]\n      \\dfrac{n+1}{2}>0, & n\\text{ odd}.\n    \\end{cases}\n   \\]\n   Hence the assumed configuration cannot exist.\n\nConsequently any admissible configuration must contain **at least two sunny lines**, i.e. \\(k\\ge 2\\).\n\n---\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}.\n\\]\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** if its direction is different from the three\nforbidden directions:\n\\[\n\\mathbf d_{x}=(1,0),\\qquad \\mathbf d_{y}=(0,1),\\qquad \n\\mathbf d_{d}=(1,-1).\n\\]\nThus a **non\u2011sunny** line is parallel to one of the three families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_{x}: y=c,\\qquad \n\\mathcal F_{y}: x=c,\\qquad \n\\mathcal F_{d}: x+y=c,\n\\]\nwhere \\(c\\in\\mathbb R\\).\n\nAssume, for a contradiction, that there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines satisfying the covering condition and that at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is sunny.\nHence at least \\(n-1\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) belong to \\(\\mathcal F_{x}\\cup\\mathcal F_{y}\\cup\\mathcal F_{d}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Maximum points covered by a non\u