            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be fixed.  \nWe shall prove that for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and having exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Consequently the set of admissible \\(k\\) is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. The sunny lines\n\nFor every integer \\(t\\) with \\(0\\le t\\le k-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell^{\\text{sun}}_{t}:\\; y = x + t\\;}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell^{\\text{sun}}_{t}\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor the line \\(x+y=0\\).  Hence every \\(\\ell^{\\text{sun}}_{t}\\) is *sunny*.  \nThe \\(k\\) lines are distinct because they have different intercepts.\n\nA lattice point \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell^{\\text{sun}}_{t}\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb-a = t . \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus the union  \n\n\\[\nS_k:=\\bigcup_{t=0}^{k-1}\\ell^{\\text{sun}}_{t}\n\\]\n\ncontains **all** points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\), \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Points that are not on a sunny line\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points required by the problem.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) fails to belong to \\(S_k\\) exactly when  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\ge k . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWrite \\(j:=b\\).  Condition (3) together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields the system  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nk+1\\le j\\le n,\\\\[2pt]\n1\\le a,\\\\[2pt]\na\\le j-k,\\\\[2pt]\na\\le n+1-j .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, \\(b-a\\ge k\\) gives \\(a\\le j-k\\); the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives \\(a\\le n+1-j\\); and because \\(b=j\\ge k+1\\) we have \\(j\\le n\\).\n\nConsequently the set of points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that are **not** already covered by the sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nT_k:=\\bigl\\{(a,j)\\mid k+1\\le j\\le n,\\;1\\le a\\le \\min\\{j-k,\\;n+1-j\\}\\bigr\\}.}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Why (5) is correct.*  \nIf \\((a,j)\\) satisfies the inequalities in (4) then it belongs to \\(\\mathcal P\\) and fulfills \\(j-a\\ge k\\), so it is not in \\(S_k\\); hence it lies in \\(T_k\\).  \nConversely, any point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) with \\(j-a\\ge k\\) must satisfy (4), and therefore belongs to the set described in (5).  Thus (5) is precisely the complement of \\(S_k\\) inside \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Horizontal lines covering the remaining points\n\nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(k+1\\le j\\le n\\) introduce the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{j}:\\; y=j\\;}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_k\\) has second coordinate \\(j\\) and therefore lies on the corresponding \\(\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{j}\\).  \nThe lines \\(\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{j}\\) are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, so none of them is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. The whole family of lines\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L\n   =\\bigl\\{\\ell^{\\text{sun}}_{t}\\mid 0\\le t\\le k-1\\bigr\\}\n    \\cup\n    \\bigl\\{\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{j}\\mid k+1\\le j\\le n\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Number of lines.*  \nThe two sub\u2011collections are disjoint, hence  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L| = k + (n-k)=n .\n\\]\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nExactly the first \\(k\\) lines have slope \\(1\\); the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are horizontal.  Therefore \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Coverage of required points.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(b-a\\le k-1\\) then, by (2), \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell^{\\text{sun}}_{\\,b-a}\\in\\mathcal L\\).\n* If \\(b-a\\ge k\\) then \\((a,b)\\in T_k\\) by definition, and by (6) it lies on the horizontal line \\(\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{b}\\in\\mathcal L\\).\n\nThus every point demanded by the problem belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Extreme values of \\(k\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The construction yields only the horizontal lines \\(y=1, y=2,\\dots ,y=n\\); none is sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\ge 0\\), so it lies on the appropriate horizontal line.\n\n* **\\(k=n\\).**  \n  The construction yields the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(y=x, y=x+1,\\dots ,y=x+n-1\\); there are no horizontal lines, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\le n-1\\), hence lies on one of them.\n\nBoth extremes are therefore admissible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* contain every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\);\n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\u220e\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
