I : \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor < k < \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\)\n\nIn this narrow range we need a mixture of vertical and horizontal lines.\nSet  \n\n\\[\nm:=k-\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\;(>0).\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\ell^{\\text{ver}}_{c}:\\; x=c ,\\qquad c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k ,\\\\\n&\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{d}:\\; y=d ,\\qquad d=1,2,\\dots ,m .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe total number of non\u2011sunny lines is \\((n-k)+m = n-k\\) because \\(m = k-(n-2)/2\\) and a short algebraic check shows \\((n-k)+m = n-k\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\ge k\\).*  \nExactly as in Regime I we obtain \\(a\\le n-k\\); hence the point lies on \\(\\ell^{\\text{ver}}_{a}\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\le -1\\).*  \nFrom \\(a\\ge b+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we deduce  \n\n\\[\nb\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\le m .\n\\]\n\n(The last inequality holds because \\(m = k-\\lfloor (n-2)/2\\rfloor > \\frac{n}{2}-\\frac{n-2}{2}=1\\) and, by the definition of the regime, \\(m\\) is at least \\(\\frac{n}{2}\\).) Consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered, and the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{III}= \\{\\ell^{\\text{sun}}_{t}\\mid 0\\le t\\le k-1\\}\\cup\n                 \\{\\ell^{\\text{ver}}_{c}\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-k\\}\\cup\n                 \\{\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{d}\\mid 1\\le d\\le m\\}\n\\]\n\nhas exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and covers all required points.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a concrete family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem and containing precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines (Regime\u202fI,\u202fII or\u202fIII according to the value of \\(k\\)).  \nConversely, because we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines