es on some \\(\\ell^{\\text{sun}}_{t}\\) iff \\(b-a\\) equals one of the integers \\(0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\).\n\n4. **Regime\u202fI (\\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\))**  \n   * Use of vertical lines \\(\\ell^{\\text{ver}}_{c}: x=c\\) for \\(c=1,\\dots ,n-k\\) \u2013 fine (they are non\u2011sunny).  \n   * **Case \\(b-a\\ge k\\):**  \n     From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge a+k\\) we obtain \\(2a+k\\le n+1\\) \u21d2 \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\).  \n     Since \\(k\\le\\frac{n-2}{2}\\), \\(\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k\\). Hence \\(a\\le n-k\\) and the point lies on \\(\\ell^{\\text{ver}}_{a}\\). The reasoning is valid.  \n   * **Case \\(b-a\\le -1\\):**  \n     Here \\(a\\ge b+1\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get \\(2b+1\\le n+1\\) \u21d2 \\(b\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\).  \n     Because \\(k\\le\\frac{n-2}{2}\\), we have \\(n-k\\ge\\frac{n}{2}+1\\), so \\(a\\le n-k\\) holds and the point again lies on \\(\\ell^{\\text{ver}}_{a}\\). The argument is acceptable.  \n   * The family \\(\\mathcal L_{I}\\) indeed contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) sunny.  \n   **Conclusion:** Regime\u202fI is correct.\n\n5. **Regime\u202fII (\\(\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\))**  \n   * Use of horizontal lines \\(\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{d}: y=d\\) for \\(d=1,\\dots ,n-k\\) \u2013 fine (non\u2011sunny).  \n   * **Case \\(b-a\\le -1\\):**  \n     From \\(a\\ge b+1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(2b+1\\le n+1\\) \u21d2 \\(b\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\).  \n     Since \\(k\\ge\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\rceil\\), we have \\(n-k\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\), thus \\(b\\le n-k\\) and the point lies on \\(\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{b}\\). Correct.  \n   * **Case \\(b-a\\ge k\\):**  \n     From \\(b\\ge a+k\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get \\(2b-k\\le n+1\\) \u21d2 \\(b\\le\\frac{n+1+k}{2}\\).  \n     Because \\(k\\ge\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\rceil\\), one checks that \\(\\frac{n+1+k}{2}\\le n-k\\). Hence \\(b\\le n-k\\) and the point lies on \\(\\ell^{\\text{hor}}_{b}\\). The derivation is valid.  \n   * 