s on one of the \\(V_c\\).\n\n*Suppose \\(b-a\\ge k\\).*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le n+1-b\\le n+1-(a+k)=n-k-a+1,\n\\]\n\nhence \\(a\\le n-k\\). Therefore \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n*Suppose \\(b-a\\le -1\\).*  \nThen \\(b\\le a-1\\). Using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get  \n\n\\[\n2b\\le a+b-1\\le n,\n\\qquad\\text{so } b\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), we have \\(b\\le k\\le n-k\\).  \nNow \\(a=b+(a-b)\\le b+(k-1)\\le n-k\\), so again \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered by the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_A=\\{S_t\\mid 0\\le t\\le k-1\\}\\cup\\{V_c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-k\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of them sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Case\u202fB : \\(\\displaystyle\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nAdd the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_d:\\; y = d ,\\qquad d=1,2,\\dots ,n-k . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny (they are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \nWe verify that they cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) that are not on a sunny line.\n\n*Suppose \\(b-a\\le -1\\).*  \nThen \\(b\\le a-1\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\le n-k,\n\\]\n\nthe last inequality because \\(k\\ge\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\n*Suppose \\(b-a\\ge k\\).*  \nThen \\(b\\ge a+k\\ge k+1\\). Together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nb\\le n+1-a\\le n+1-(b-k)=n-k+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(b\\le n-k\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(H_b\\).\n\nTherefore every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered by  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_B=\\{S_t\\mid 0\\le t\\le k-1\\}\\cup\\{H_d\\mid 1\\le d\\le n-k\\},\n\\]\n\nagain a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n--------------------------------