---------------------------------------------\n### 3. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nv&=\\#\\{\\text{vertical non\u2011sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\\\\nh&=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal non\u2011sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\\\\nd&=\\#\\{\\text{non\u2011sunny lines of slope }-1\\text{ in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nv+h+d=n-k. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.1 Points with \\(b-a\\ge k\\)\n\nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge a+k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a\\le n+1-k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThus every such point has its \\(x\\)-coordinate in the set  \n\\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\rfloor\\}\\).  \nA vertical line fixes the \\(x\\)-coordinate, so covering all these points requires at least  \n\n\\[\nv\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.2 Points with \\(b-a\\le -1\\)\n\nHere \\(b\\le a-1\\). Together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get  \n\n\\[\n2b\\le n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b\\le\\frac{n}{2}. \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(y\\)-coordinate of any such point belongs to  \n\\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\).  \nA point with a given \\(y\\)-value can be covered either by a horizontal line (which fixes \\(y\\)) or by a line of slope \\(-1\\) (which fixes the sum \\(x+y\\)).  \nSince the possible values of \\(y\\) are exactly \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) distinct numbers, at least that many lines of the two latter types are needed:\n\n\\[\nh+d\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.3 Combining the inequalities\n\nFrom (3.1), (3.3) and (3.5),\n\n\\[\nn-k=v+h+d\\ge\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nA short case analysis on the parity of \\(n\\) yields the equivalent condition\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{3.6}\n\\]\n\nThus **no** configuration with more than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines can satisfy the covering requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  Use the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_t\\) defined in (2.1).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  Take the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k). \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(V_c\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny, and they are distinct.  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{S_t\\mid 0\\le t\\le k-1\\}\\cup\\{V_c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n**Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P\\).**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n1. If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then by (2.2) \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\n2. If \\(b-a\\ge k\\), inequality (3.2) gives \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\).  \n   Because \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) we have \\(\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k\\); hence \\(a\\le n-k\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n3. If \\(b-a\\le -1\\) then \\(b\\le a-1\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le\\frac{n+2}{2}\\).  \n   Again \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) implies \\(\\frac{n+2}{2}\\le n-k\\), so \\(a\\le n-k\\) and \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(V_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nThe inequality (3.6) shows that a configuration can exist only when  \n\\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nConversely, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) the explicit family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) constructed above satisfies 