n for numbers of non\u2011sunny lines (v,\u202fh,\u202fd) and equation (3.1).**  \nCorrect.\n\n**Step 7 \u2013 Derivation of inequality (3.2).**  \nFrom \\(b\\ge a+k\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(2a\\le n+1-k\\); this is correct.\n\n**Step 8 \u2013 Claim (3.3): \u201cA vertical line fixes the \\(x\\)-coordinate, so covering all these points requires at least  \n\\(v\\ge\\lceil (n+1-k)/2\\rceil\\).\u201d**  \n**Critical Error.**  \nThe set of points with \\(b-a\\ge k\\) indeed has \\(x\\)-coordinates in \\(\\{1,\\dots,\\lfloor (n+1-k)/2\\rfloor\\}\\). However, the argument assumes that each distinct \\(x\\)-value must be covered by a different vertical line. This is false because a non\u2011sunny line of slope \\(-1\\) (or even a sunny line of another slope) can also pass through points with different \\(x\\)-coordinates while still satisfying the covering requirement. Consequently, the lower bound on the number of vertical lines \\(v\\) is not justified; the inequality (3.3) may be too large. Since the subsequent bound on \\(k\\) relies on (3.3), the whole upper\u2011bound argument collapses.\n\n*Because this is a critical error, any later conclusions that depend on (3.3) are not validated. We therefore stop checking the remainder of the \u201cupper bound\u201d section.*\n\n**Step 9 \u2013 (Skipped) Derivation of (3.4) and (3.5).**  \nAlthough (3.4) is correct, the subsequent inequality (3.5) suffers from a **Justification Gap**: it treats each possible \\(y\\)-value as requiring a distinct horizontal or slope\u2011\\(-1\\) line, ignoring that a single slope\u2011\\(-1\\) line can cover many different \\(y\\)-values (all points with the same sum \\(x+y\\)). The argument does not prove that \\(\\lceil n/2\\rceil\\) lines are necessary, so the bound is not rigorously established.\n\n**Step 10 \u2013 Combination of inequalities leading to (3.6).**  \nSince (3.3) is invalid and (3.5) is not rigorously justified, the deduction that \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)