mber of non\u2011sunny lines of slope \\(-1\\) (i.e. parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n   Then  \n\n   \\[\n   v+h+d=n-k. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Covering the region \\(b-a\\ge k\\).*  \n   From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge a+k\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence every point with \\(b-a\\ge k\\) has its \\(x\\)-coordinate in the set  \n   \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\rfloor\\}\\).  \n   To cover all of them we need at least  \n\n   \\[\n   v\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   *Covering the region \\(b-a\\le -1\\).*  \n   Here \\(b\\le a-1\\). Together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get  \n\n   \\[\n   b\\le\\frac{n}{2}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus every point with \\(b-a\\le-1\\) has its \\(y\\)-coordinate in  \n   \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\).  \n   They can be covered either by a horizontal line or by a line of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   A horizontal line fixes the \\(y\\)-coordinate, while a line of slope \\(-1\\) fixes the sum \\(x+y\\).  \n   For a point with \\(b\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) the value of \\(b\\) determines a unique horizontal line, and the value of \\(a+b\\) (which lies between \\(2\\) and \\(n+1\\)) determines a unique slope\u2011\\(-1\\) line.  \n   Consequently, to guarantee coverage of **all** such points we need at least  \n\n   \\[\n   h+d\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Combining (1), (3) and (5) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k=v+h+d\\ge\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+\n   \\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n   \\]\n\n   A straightforward parity analysis (separating the cases \\(n\\) even and \\(n\\) odd) reduces the inequality to  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence **no** configuration with more than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines can satisfy the covering requirement.\n\n5. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n   *Sunny lines:* the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_t\\) from step\u202f2.\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny lines:* use only vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n   \\]\n\n   (All are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny, and they are distinct.)\n\n   **Verification.**  \n   Take any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).\n\n   - If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{\\,b-a}\\) by (C).  \n   - If \\(b-a\\ge k\\), inequality (2) yields \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k\\) (the last inequality follows from \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \n   - If \\(b-a\\le-1\\) then \\(b\\le a-1\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le\\frac{n+2}{2}\\). Because \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) we have \\(\\frac{n+2}{2}\\le n-k\\); thus again \\(a\\le n-k\\) and \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(V_a\\).\n\n   Therefore every required lattice point is covered by at least one line of the family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\\{S_t\\mid 0\\le t\\le k-1\\}\\cup\\{V_c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-k\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (6) and the explicit construction for each \\(k\\) satisfying it together prove that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Sunny and non\u2011sunny lines\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is **not** parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nHence a **non\u2011sunny** line is parallel to at least one of those directions; it may be\n\n* horizontal (slope \\(0\\));\n* vertical (undefined slope);\n* of slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. A convenient family of sunny lines\n\nFor an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\;y=x+t\\qquad(t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1). \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_t\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore it is sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) lies on some \\(S_t\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) not covered by the sunny lines satisfy  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\ge k\\quad\\text{or}\\quad b-a\\le -1 . \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers \\(\\mathcal P\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nv&=\\#\\{\\text{vertical non\u2011sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\\\\nh&=\\#\\{\\text{horizontal non\u2011sunny lines in }\\mathcal L\\},\\\\\nd&=\\#\\{\\text{non\u2011sunny lines of slope }-1\\text{ in }\\mathcal L\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nv+h+d=n-k. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.1 Points with \\(b-a\\ge k\\)\n\nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge a+k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n2a\\le n+1-k\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nThus every such point has its \\(x\\)-coordinate in the set  \n\\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\rfloor\\}\\).  \nA vertical line fixes the \\(x\\)-coordinate, so covering all these points requires at least  \n\n\\[\nv\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.2 Points with \\(b-a\\le -1\\)\n\nHere \\(b\\le a-1\\). Together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we get  \n\n\\[\n2b\\le n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad b\\le\\frac{n}{2}. \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\nHence the \\(y\\)-coordinate of any such point belongs to  \n\\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\).  \nA point with a given \\(y\\)-value can be covered either by a horizontal line (which fixes \\(y\\)) or by a line of slope \\(-1\\) (which fixes the sum \\(x+y\\)).  \nSince the possible values of \\(y\\) are exactly \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) distinct numbers, at least that many lines of the two latter types are needed:\n\n\\[\nh+d\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil . \\tag{3.5}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.3 Combining the inequalities\n\nFrom (3.1), (3.3) and (3.5),\n\n\\[\nn-k=v+h+d\\ge\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil+\n\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nA short case analysis on the parity of \\(n\\) yields the equivalent condition\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{3.6}\n\\]\n\nThus **no** configuration with more than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines can satisfy the covering requirement.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  Use the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_t\\) defined in (2.1).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  Take the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_c:\\;x=c\\qquad(c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k). \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nAll \\(V_c\\) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny, and they are distinct.  \nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\\{S_t\\mid 0\\le t\\le k-1\\}\\cup\\{V_c\\mid 1\\le c\\le n-k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n**Verification that \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) covers \\(\\mathcal P\\).**  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n1. If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then by (2.2) \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{\\,b-a}\\).\n\n2. If \\(b-a\\ge k\\), inequality (3.2) gives \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\).  \n   Because \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) we have \\(\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k\\); hence \\(a\\le n-k\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n\n3. If \\(b-a\\le -1\\) then \\(b\\le a-1\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le\\frac{n+2}{2}\\).  \n   Again \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) implies \\(\\frac{n+2}{2}\\le n-k\\), so \\(a\\le n-k\\) and \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(V_a\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Conclusion\n\nThe inequality (3.6) shows that a configuration can exist only when  \n\\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nConversely, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) the explicit family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) constructed above satisfies all the requirements.  \n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}} .\n\\]"
