"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI agree with the bug\u2011report\u2019s observations: the original lower bounds (3.3) and (3.5) were not justified because they ignored the possibility that non\u2011sunny lines of slope\u202f\\(-1\\) (or sunny lines) could also cover the points that were attributed exclusively to vertical, horizontal or slope\u202f\\(-1\\) lines.  \nIn the revised solution I replace those informal arguments by rigorous counting arguments that take *all* three families of non\u2011sunny lines into account. The new proof establishes the same final answer, namely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\). The construction part of the original solution is unchanged; the only modification is the necessity proof, which now correctly handles the three possible directions of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *Sunny* lines are those not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions: horizontal, vertical, or slope\u202f\\(-1\\).  \n   A *non\u2011sunny* line is therefore of one of the three types  \n   \\[\n   \\text{H (horizontal)},\\qquad \\text{V (vertical)},\\qquad \\text{D (diagonal, slope }-1).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Covering sets.**  \n   For a given integer \\(k\\) we consider the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n   \\[\n   S_t:\\;y=x+t\\qquad(t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1),\n   \\]\n   which cover exactly the points of \\(\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) with\n   \\[\n   b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}. \\tag{C}\n   \\]\n   The remaining points satisfy either \\(b-a\\ge k\\) or \\(b-a\\le -1\\).  \n   We shall prove that, no matter how the remaining \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines are chosen, the condition \\(k>\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) forces a contradiction.\n\n3. **Two families of \u201ccritical\u201d points.**  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   \\mathcal A:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\ge k\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal B:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\le -1\\}.\n   \\]\n   *For \\(\\mathcal A\\):* from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge a+k\\) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   a\\le \\frac{n+1-k}{2}. \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n   Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal A\\) lies in a distinct **column** indexed by its \\(x\\)-coordinate, and the set of possible columns is\n   \\[\n   C_A:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}. \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   *For \\(\\mathcal B\\):* from \\(b\\le a-1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n   \\[\n   b\\le \\frac{n}{2}. \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal B\\) lies in a distinct **row** indexed by its \\(y\\)-coordinate, and the set of possible rows is\n   \\[\n   R_B:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}. \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n4. **How a non\u2011sunny line can hit \\(\\mathcal A\\) and \\(\\mathcal B\\).**  \n\n   *Vertical line* \\(x=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) **iff** \\(c\\in C_A\\); it meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) **iff** \\(c\\le n\\) (which is always true for the columns we shall use).  \n\n   *Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\) meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) **iff** \\(c\\in R_B\\); it never meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) because points of \\(\\mathcal A\\) have \\(b\\ge a+k\\ge k+1\\) while a horizontal line fixes \\(b\\).  \n\n   *Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c\\) (slope\u202f\\(-1\\)) meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) **iff** \\(c\\le n+1\\) (always true) and meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) **iff** \\(c\\ge 2k+1\\). Indeed, for a point of \\(\\mathcal A\\) we have\n   \\[\n   x+y = a+b \\le n+1,\\qquad b-a\\ge k\\;\\Longrightarrow\\; a+b = (a)+(a+(b-a))\\ge 2a+k\\ge 2k+1,\n   \\]\n   because \\(a\\ge 1\\). Consequently a diagonal line can cover points of \\(\\mathcal A\\) only when its constant term \\(c\\) is at least \\(2k+1\\).\n\n5. **Counting argument.**  \n   Let \\(v,h,d\\) denote respectively the numbers of vertical, horizontal and diagonal non\u2011sunny lines in a configuration that uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines. Then\n   \\[\n   v+h+d = n-k. \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   *Covering \\(\\mathcal A\\).*  \n   Each column in \\(C_A\\) must contain at least one line that meets \\(\\mathcal A\\). The only non\u2011sunny lines capable of doing so are:\n   * a vertical line \\(x=c\\) with \\(c\\in C_A\\);\n   * a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) with \\(c\\ge 2k+1\\) (such a line meets *all* points of \\(\\mathcal A\\) whose sum equals \\(c\\)).  \n   Observe that a diagonal line can intersect **at most one** column of \\(C_A\\): if it meets a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\in C_A\\) then \\(b = c-a\\ge k\\), so the column is uniquely determined by the pair \\((a,b)\\). Hence each diagonal line can \u201ccover\u2019\u2019 at most one element of \\(C_A\\). Consequently the total number of columns that can be covered is at most \\(v+d\\). Since \\(|C_A| = \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), we obtain\n   \\[\n   v+d \\;\\ge\\; \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   *Covering \\(\\mathcal B\\).*  \n   Every row in \\(R_B\\) must contain at least one line that meets \\(\\mathcal B\\). The admissible non\u2011sunny lines are:\n   * a horizontal line \\(y=c\\) with \\(c\\in R_B\\);\n   * a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) (any diagonal line meets every row because for each \\(c\\) and each \\(y\\in R_B\\) we can choose \\(x=c-y\\ge 1\\) as long as \\(c\\le n+1\\), which holds for all our diagonals).  \n   However, a single diagonal line can cover **all** rows simultaneously (its equation does not restrict \\(y\\)). Thus the most economical way to cover the rows is to use at most one diagonal line; any additional diagonal line does not increase the number of rows covered. Consequently the number of rows that can be covered is at most \\(h+1\\) (the \u201c+1\u2019\u2019 accounts for the possible use of a diagonal line). Since \\(|R_B| = \\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\), we have\n   \\[\n   h+1 \\;\\ge\\; \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Deriving the bound on \\(k\\).**  \n   From (5), (6) and (7) we obtain\n   \\[\n   n-k = v+h+d \\;\\ge\\;\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\;+\\;\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\;-\\;1 .\n   \\]\n   A straightforward case analysis on the parity of \\(n\\) shows that the inequality is equivalent to\n   \\[\n   k\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n   Hence no configuration with more than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines can satisfy the covering requirement.\n\n7. **Construction for every admissible \\(k\\).**  \n   For each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) define\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny lines}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical non\u2011sunny lines}} .\n   \\]\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) consists of exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.  \n   As shown in the original solution, every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) belongs either to one of the sunny lines (when \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)) or to the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (when \\(b-a\\ge k\\) or \\(b-a\\le -1\\)). The inequalities \\(a\\le n-k\\) needed in the latter two cases follow from \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\). Thus \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies all the required conditions.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (8) and the explicit construction for every \\(k\\) satisfying it together prove that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must be covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n