---------------------\n### 1.  Sunny and non\u2011sunny lines\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nConsequently a **non\u2011sunny** line belongs to exactly one of the three families  \n\n* **H** \u2013 horizontal lines \\(y=c\\);\n* **V** \u2013 vertical lines \\(x=c\\);\n* **D** \u2013 diagonal lines of slope \\(-1\\), i.e. \\(x+y=c\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A convenient set of sunny lines\n\nFor an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\;y=x+t\\qquad(t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1). \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_t\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore all of them are sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) lies on some \\(S_t\\) exactly when  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nHence the points of \\(\\mathcal P\\) not covered by the sunny lines satisfy  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\ge k\\quad\\text{or}\\quad b-a\\le -1 . \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nDenote  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\ge k\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal B:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\le -1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Geometry of the two uncovered regions\n\n*For \\(\\mathcal A\\).*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge a+k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal A\\) lies in a column whose index belongs to  \n\n\\[\nC_A:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n*For \\(\\mathcal B\\).*  \nFrom \\(b\\le a-1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le\\frac{n}{2}. \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal B\\) lies in a row whose index belongs to  \n\n\\[\nR_B:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}. \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  How non\u2011sunny lines intersect \\(\\mathcal A\\) and \\(\\mathcal B\\)\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\):  \n\u2003\u2013 meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) iff \\(c\\in C_A\\);  \n\u2003\u2013 meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) for any \\(c\\) (because \\(\\mathcal B\\) contains points with every possible \\(x\\) up to \\(n\\)).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\):  \n\u2003\u2013 meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) iff \\(c\\in R_B\\);  \n\u2003\u2013 never meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) (points of \\(\\mathcal A\\) have \\(y\\ge k+1\\) while a horizontal line fixes \\(y\\)).\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)):  \n\u2003\u2013 meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) for any \\(c\\le n+1\\) (all such lines are present in our family D);  \n\u2003\u2013 meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) only when \\(c\\ge 2k+1\\). Indeed, for \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal A\\) we have  \n\\[\nc=a+b\\ge a+(a+k)=2a+k\\ge 2k+1 .\n\\]\n\nMoreover, a diagonal line can intersect **at most one** column of \\(C_A\\): if it contains a point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\in C_A\\) then \\(b=c-a\\ge k\\), so the column index \\(a\\) is uniquely determined by the pair \\((a,b)\\). Consequently each diagonal line can \u201ccover\u2019\u2019 at most one element of \\(C_A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Counting the necessary non\u2011sunny lines\n\nAssume a configuration uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nv=\\#\\text{vertical lines},\\qquad\nh=\\#\\text{horizontal lines},\\qquad\nd=\\#\\text{diagonal lines}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nv+h+d=n-k. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n#### 5.1  Covering \\(\\mathcal A\\)\n\nEvery column in \\(C_A\\) must contain at least one line that meets \\(\\mathcal A\\). The only non\u2011sunny lines capable of doing this are vertical lines (each covers exactly its own column) and diagonal lines (each can cover at most one column, as observed above). Hence\n\n\\[\nv+d\\;\\ge\\;|C_A|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\n#### 5.2  Covering \\(\\mathcal B\\)\n\nEvery row in \\(R_B\\) must contain at least one line that meets \\(\\mathcal B\\). Horizontal lines each cover exactly one row; a diagonal line covers **all** rows simultaneously (its equation does not restrict the \\(y\\)-coordinate). Therefore the rows can be covered using at most one diagonal line together with horizontal lines, which yields\n\n\\[\nh+1\\;\\ge\\;|R_B|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\n(The \u201c+1\u2019\u2019 accounts for the possible use of a diagonal line; if no diagonal line is used the inequality reduces to \\(h\\ge\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), which is still valid.)\n\n#### 5.3  Combining the inequalities\n\nFrom (5.1), (5.2) and (5.3) we obtain\n\n\\[\nn-k = v+h+d \\;\\ge\\;\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\;+\\;\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor \\;-\\;1 .\n\\]\n\nA short case analysis on the parity of \\(n\\) shows that the above inequality is equivalent to\n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{5.4}\n\\]\n\nThus **no** family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition can contain more than \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nDefine the family\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}} \\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical (non\u2011sunny)}} .\n\\]\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n*Verification.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(y=x+(b-a)\\) (by (2.2)).\n* If \\(b-a\\ge k\\) then (3.1) gives \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k\\) (the last inequality follows from \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)). Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n* If \\(b-a\\le -1\\) then \\(b\\le a-1\\). From \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain \\(a\\le\\frac{n+2}{2}\\le n-k\\) (again using \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\)). Thus \\((a,b)\\) also lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\n\nIn every case the point belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_k\\). Hence \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) satisfies both required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Final answer\n\nThe necessary condition (5.4) and the explicit construction for each \\(k\\) satisfying it together prove that, for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nare exactly the non\u2011negative integers for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\) and having precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines."
