"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nThe original construction that used only the vertical lines  \n\\(\\{x=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\) does **not** cover the points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(b-a\\le -1\\) when \\(k\\) is close to \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nConsequently the proof of existence for all admissible \\(k\\) was incomplete.  \n\nI give a **complete and rigorous solution**.  \nFirst I prove that any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThen I exhibit explicit constructions for **every** integer \\(k\\) with  \n\\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nTwo separate constructions are needed:\n\n* **Case\u202fA** \u2013 \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n  All non\u2011sunny lines are vertical; this works because every uncovered point has its \\(x\\)\u2011coordinate \\(\\le n-k\\).\n\n* **Case\u202fB** \u2013 \\(k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) (the maximal possible value).  \n  Here we use a mixture of vertical and horizontal non\u2011sunny lines; the extra horizontal lines guarantee coverage of the points with \\(b-a\\le-1\\).\n\nTogether these constructions show that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**b. Method Sketch**\n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *Sunny* lines are those not parallel to the three forbidden directions (horizontal, vertical, slope\u202f\\(-1\\)).  \n   Non\u2011sunny lines belong to one of the three families  \n   \\(\\mathbf H\\) (horizontal \\(y=c\\)), \\(\\mathbf V\\) (vertical \\(x=c\\)), \\(\\mathbf D\\) (diagonal \\(x+y=c\\)).\n\n2. **Covering set \\(\\mathcal P\\).**  \n   \\(\\displaystyle\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\\).  \n   For a given integer \\(k\\) we take the sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   S_t:\\;y=x+t\\qquad(t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1).\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   A point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) lies on some \\(S_t\\) iff \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\).  \n   The points not covered by the sunny lines split into  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal A:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\ge k\\},\\qquad\n   \\mathcal B:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\le-1\\}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Geometric description of \\(\\mathcal A,\\mathcal B\\).**  \n   From the definitions we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\quad\\text{for }(a,b)\\in\\mathcal A,\n   \\qquad\n   b\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\quad\\text{for }(a,b)\\in\\mathcal B.\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(\\mathcal A\\) occupies at most the columns  \n\n   \\[\n   C_A:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   and \\(\\mathcal B\\) occupies at most the rows  \n\n   \\[\n   R_B:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n4. **How non\u2011sunny lines can meet \\(\\mathcal A\\) and \\(\\mathcal B\\).**  \n   *Vertical* lines can meet \\(\\mathcal A\\) only by coinciding with a column of \\(C_A\\); they can meet \\(\\mathcal B\\) for any column.  \n   *Horizontal* lines can meet \\(\\mathcal B\\) only by coinciding with a row of \\(R_B\\); they never meet \\(\\mathcal A\\).  \n   *Diagonal* lines of slope \\(-1\\) can meet \\(\\mathcal B\\) for any constant term, and can meet \\(\\mathcal A\\) only when their constant term is at least \\(2k+1\\); moreover a diagonal line can intersect **at most one** column of \\(C_A\\).\n\n5. **Necessary bound on \\(k\\).**  \n   Let \\(v,h,d\\) be the numbers of vertical, horizontal and diagonal non\u2011sunny lines.  \n   Because we have exactly \\(n\\) lines,  \n\n   \\[\n   v+h+d=n-k. \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   *Covering \\(\\mathcal A\\).*  \n   Each column of \\(C_A\\) needs a line that meets \\(\\mathcal A\\); only vertical and diagonal lines can do this, and each diagonal can serve at most one column. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   v+d\\ge |C_A|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *Covering \\(\\mathcal B\\).*  \n   Each row of \\(R_B\\) needs a line that meets \\(\\mathcal B\\); horizontal lines cover exactly one row each, while a single diagonal line covers **all** rows. Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   h+1\\ge |R_B|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding (8) and (9) and using (7) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   A straightforward parity check shows that this inequality is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus (10) is a **necessary** condition.\n\n6. **Sufficient constructions.**  \n   *Case\u202fA* (\\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   The family contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\):\n   * if \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) it lies on the sunny line \\(y=x+(b-a)\\);\n   * otherwise \\(b-a\\ge k\\) or \\(b-a\\le-1\\).  \n     From (4) we have \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k\\) when \\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\); similarly, using \\(b\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\le n-k\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n-k\\) for the case \\(b-a\\le-1\\).  \n     Hence the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to the set \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\)) contains the point.  \n   Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered.\n\n   *Case\u202fB* (\\(k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)).  \n   Write \\(k=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\