n\u2003\u2013 meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) for any admissible constant \\(c\\le n+1\\);  \n\u2003\u2013 meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) only when \\(c\\ge 2k+1\\).  \nMoreover a diagonal line can intersect **at most one** column of \\(C_A\\); if it contains a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal A\\) then that column is uniquely \\(a\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  A necessary bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a configuration uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nv=\\#\\text{vertical lines},\\qquad\nh=\\#\\text{horizontal lines},\\qquad\nd=\\#\\text{diagonal lines}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nv+h+d=n-k. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n**Covering \\(\\mathcal A\\).**  \nEvery column of \\(C_A\\) must contain a line meeting \\(\\mathcal A\\). Only vertical and diagonal lines can do this, and each diagonal can serve at most one column. Hence  \n\n\\[\nv+d\\ge |C_A|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\n**Covering \\(\\mathcal B\\).**  \nEvery row of \\(R_B\\) must contain a line meeting \\(\\mathcal B\\). Horizontal lines each cover exactly one row, while a single diagonal line covers *all* rows. Consequently  \n\n\\[\nh+1\\ge |R_B|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\nAdding (5.2) and (5.3) and using (5.1) gives  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1 .\n\\]\n\nA routine parity check shows that this inequality is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}. \\tag{5.4}\n\\]\n\nThus (5.4) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Constructions for all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.1  Cas