e at most one column. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   v+d\\ge |C_A|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   *Covering \\(\\mathcal B\\).*  \n   Each row of \\(R_B\\) needs a line that meets \\(\\mathcal B\\); horizontal lines cover exactly one row each, while a single diagonal line covers **all** rows. Consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   h+1\\ge |R_B|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding (8) and (9) and using (7) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   A straightforward parity check shows that this inequality is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus (10) is a **necessary** condition.\n\n6. **Sufficient constructions.**  \n   *Case\u202fA* (\\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   The family contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\):\n   * if \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) it lies on the sunny line \\(y=x+(b-a)\\);\n   * otherwise \\(b-a\\ge k\\) or \\(b-a\\le-1\\).  \n     From (4) we have \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k\\) when \\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\); similarly, using \\(b\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\le n-k\\) we obtain \\(a\\le n-k\\) for the case \\(b-a\\le-1\\).  \n     Hence the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to the set \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\)) contains the point.  \n   Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered.\n\n   *Case\u202fB* (\\(k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)).  \n   Write \\(k=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) and set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\underbrace{\