mathbf H:\\;y=c,\\qquad\n\\mathbf V:\\;x=c,\\qquad\n\\mathbf D:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A convenient set of sunny lines\n\nFor an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\;y=x+t\\qquad(t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1). \\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(S_t\\) has slope \\(1\\); therefore all of them are sunny.  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) lies on some \\(S_t\\) exactly when  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}. \\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nHence the points not covered by the sunny lines satisfy  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\ge k\\quad\\text{or}\\quad b-a\\le -1 . \\tag{2.3}\n\\]\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\ge k\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal B:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\le -1\\}. \\tag{2.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Geometry of \\(\\mathcal A\\) and \\(\\mathcal B\\)\n\n*For \\(\\mathcal A\\).*  \nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(b\\ge a+k\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal A\\) lies in a column whose index belongs to  \n\n\\[\nC_A:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}. \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\n*For \\(\\mathcal B\\).*  \nFrom \\(b\\le a-1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nb\\le\\frac{n}{2}. \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal B\\) lies in a row whose index belongs to  \n\n\\[\nR_B:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}. \\tag{3.4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Interaction with non\u2011sunny lines\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\):  \n\u2003\u2013 meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) iff \\(c\\in C_A\\);  \n\u2003\u2013 meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) for any \\(c\\).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\):  \n\u2003\u2013 meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) iff \\(c\\in R_B\\);  \n\u2003\u2013 never meets \\(\\mathcal A\\).\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)):  \n\u2003\u2013 meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) for any admissible constant \\(c\\le n+1\\);  \n\u2003\u2013 meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) only when \\(c\\ge 2k+1\\).  \nMoreover a diagonal line can intersect **at most one** column of \\(C_A\\); if it contains a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal A\\) then that column is uniquely \\(a\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  A necessary bound on the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume a configuration uses exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nv=\\#\\text{vertical lines},\\qquad\nh=\\#\\text{horizontal lines},\\qquad\nd=\\#\\text{diagonal lines}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\nv+h+d=n-k. \\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n**Covering \\(\\mathcal A\\).**  \nEvery column of \\(C_A\\) must contain a line meeting \\(\\mathcal A\\). Only vertical and diagonal lines can do this, and each diagonal can serve at most one column. Hence  \n\n\\[\nv+d\\ge |C_A|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\n**Covering \\(\\mathcal B\\).**  \nEvery row of \\(R_B\\) must contain a line meeting \\(\\mathcal B\\). Horizontal lines each cover exactly one row, while a single diagonal line covers *all* rows. Consequently  \n\n\\[\nh+1\\ge |R_B|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{5.3}\n\\]\n\nAdding (5.2) and (5.3) and using (5.1) gives  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1 .\n\\]\n\nA routine parity check shows that this inequality is equivalent to  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}. \\tag{5.4}\n\\]\n\nThus (5.4) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of a suitable family of \\(n\\) lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Constructions for all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.1  Case\u202fA : \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} .\n\\tag{6.1}\n\\]\n\nThe family contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n*Verification.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(y=x+(b-a)\\).\n\n* Otherwise \\(b-a\\ge k\\) or \\(b-a\\le-1\\).  \n  From (3.1) we have \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\). Because \\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\),\n\n  \\[\n  \\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k,\n  \\]\n\n  so \\(a\\le n-k\\) and the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to the set \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\)) contains the point.  \n  The same inequality also guarantees \\(a\\le n-k\\) when \\(b-a\\le-1\\) (using \\(b\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\le n-k\\)).  \n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.2  Case\u202fB : \\(k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) (the maximal possible value)\n\nWrite \\(k=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\). Set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-2k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,k\\,\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}} .\n\\tag{6.2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n-2k\\ge0\\) (equality when \\(n\\) is even), the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-2k)+k=n .\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \nAgain let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) the point lies on a sunny line.\n\n* If \\(b-a\\ge k\\) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n  \\[\n  a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}=k+1\\quad(\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}),\\qquad\n  a\\le k\\quad(\\text{if }n\\text{ is even}).\n  \\]\n\n  In both sub\u2011cases \\(a\\le n-2k\\); therefore the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in the construction) contains the point.\n\n* If \\(b-a\\le-1\\) then \\(b\\le\\frac{n}{2}=k\\). Hence the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which belongs to \\(\\{y=1,\\dots ,k\\}\\)) contains the point.\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  Conclusion\n\nInequality (5.4) shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nConversely, the explicit families (6.1) and (6.2) provide, for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nHence the complete answer to the problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
