\) and \\(\\mathcal B\\).**  \n\n   *Vertical* line \\(x=c\\): meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) iff \\(c\\in C_A\\); meets every point of \\(\\mathcal B\\) (because \\(\\mathcal B\\) contains points with any admissible \\(x\\)).  \n\n   *Horizontal* line \\(y=c\\): meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) iff \\(c\\in R_B\\); never meets \\(\\mathcal A\\).  \n\n   *Diagonal* line \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)):  \n\n   \u2013 For any integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the line contains at least one point of \\(\\mathcal B\\) (take \\(a=1,\\;b=c-1\\); then \\(a+b=c\\le n+1\\) and \\(b-a=c-2\\ge0\\), so \\((1,c-1)\\in\\mathcal B\\)).  \n\n   \u2013 It meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) only when \\(c\\ge 2k+1\\); in that case it can contain points from **at most one** column of \\(C_A\\) (if \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal A\\) lies on the diagonal then \\(a=c-b\\) is uniquely determined).\n\n6. **Necessary bound on \\(k\\).**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   v=\\#\\text{vertical lines},\\;\n   h=\\#\\text{horizontal lines},\\;\n   d=\\#\\text{diagonal lines}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines,  \n\n   \\[\n   v+h+d=n-k. \\tag{6.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Covering \\(\\mathcal A\\).*  \n   Every column of \\(C_A\\) must contain a line that meets \\(\\mathcal A\\). Only vertical and diagonal lines can do this, and each diagonal can serve at most one column. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   v+d\\ge |C_A|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{6.2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Covering \\(\\mathcal B\\).*  \n   Every row of \\(R_B\\) must contain a line that meets \\(\\mathcal B\\). Horizontal lines each cover exactly one row; a diagonal line (if present) covers **all** rows of \\(R_B\\). Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   h+\\mathbf 1_{\\{d\\ge 1\\}}\\ge |R_B|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{6.3}\n   \\]\n\n   (Here \\(\\mathbf 1_{\\{d\\ge 1\\}}\\) equals\u202f1 when at least one diagonal line is used and\u202f0 otherwise.)\n\n   Adding (6.2) and (6.3) and using (6.1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge\n  