n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-\n   \\mathbf 1_{\\{d\\ge 1\\}} .\n   \\tag{6.4}\n   \\]\n\n   The right\u2011hand side is maximal when \\(\\mathbf 1_{\\{d\\ge 1\\}}=0\\); thus (6.4) implies  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1 .\n   \\]\n\n   A straightforward case analysis on the parity of \\(n\\) and of \\(k\\) shows that the above inequality is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}. \\tag{6.5}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence (6.5) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of a suitable configuration.\n\n7. **Sufficient constructions.**  \n   We now exhibit, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n   *Case\u202fA* \u2013 \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} .\n   \\tag{7.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The family contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n   \u2013 If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(y=x+(b-a)\\).\n\n   \u2013 Otherwise \\(b-a\\ge k\\) or \\(b-a\\le-1\\).  \n     From (3.3) we have \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\). Because \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\),\n\n     \\[\n     \\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k,\n     \\tag{7.2}\n     \\]\n\n     (indeed \\(2(n-k)- (n+1-k)=n-1-k\\ge 1\\)).  \n     Hence \\(a\\le n-k\\) and the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to the set \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\)) contains the point.  \n     The same inequality also guarantees \\(a\\le n-k\\) when \\(b-a\\le-1\\) (using \\(b\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\le n-k\\)).  \n     Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered.\n\n   *Case\u202fB* \u2013 \\(k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) (the maximal admissible value).  \n   Write \\(k=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\). Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-2k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,y=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,k\\,\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}} .\n   \\tag{7.3}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(n-2k\\ge 0\\) (equality when \\(n\\) is even), the total number of lines is  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-2k)+k=n .\n   \\]\n\n   *Verification.*  \n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n   \u2013 If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) the point lies on a sunny line as before.\n\n   \u2013 If \\(b-a\\ge k\\) then, using \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n     \\[\n     a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}.\n     \\tag{7.4}\n     \\]\n\n     When \\(n\\) is even, \\(n=2k\\) and (7.4) gives \\(a\\le k=n-2k\\); thus the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present because we included all \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-2k\\)) contains the point.\n\n     When \\(n\\) is odd, \\(n=2k+1\\) and (7.4) yields \\(a\\le k+1\\). In this case \\(n-2k=1\\); the only vertical line we have is \\(x=1\\). If \\(a=1\\) we are done. If \\(a\\ge2\\) we use a horizontal line: from \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and \\(b-a\\ge k\\) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     b\\le n+1-a\\le (2k+2)-a\\le k .\n     \\]\n\n     Hence \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which belongs to the set \\(\\{y=1,\\dots ,k\\}\\)) contains the point.  \n\n   \u2013 If \\(b-a\\le-1\\) then \\(b\\le\\frac{n}{2}=k\\); consequently the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (present in the construction) contains the point.\n\n   Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered in all sub\u2011cases.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (6.5) is necessary, and the explicit families (7.1) and (7.3) show that it is also sufficient. Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**2. Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nConsequently a **non\u2011sunny** line belongs to exactly one of the families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf H:\\;y=c,\\qquad\n\\mathbf V:\\;x=c,\\qquad\n\\mathbf D:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Choice of sunny lines\n\nFor a given integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we take the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\;y=x+t\\qquad(t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) lies on some \\(S_t\\) iff  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDefine the two subsets of points not covered by the sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\ge k\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal B:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\le-1\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Geometry of \\(\\mathcal A\\) and \\(\\mathcal B\\)\n\nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and the definitions (3) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(a,b)\\in\\mathcal A &\\Longrightarrow a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2},\\\\[2pt]\n(a,b)\\in\\mathcal B &\\Longrightarrow b\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence all points of \\(\\mathcal A\\) lie in the set of columns  \n\n\\[\nC_A:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}, \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nand all points of \\(\\mathcal B\\) lie in the set of rows  \n\n\\[\nR_B:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Interaction with non\u2011sunny lines\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\): meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) iff \\(c\\in C_A\\); it meets every point of \\(\\mathcal B\\) (because \\(\\mathcal B\\) contains points with any admissible \\(x\\)).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\): meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) iff \\(c\\in R_B\\); it never meets \\(\\mathcal A\\).\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)):  \n\n* For any integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the point \\((1,c-1)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal B\\) (it satisfies \\(1+(c-1)=c\\le n+1\\) and \\(c-1-1=c-2\\ge0\\)), so the diagonal contains at least one point of \\(\\mathcal B\\).  \n\n* It can contain points of \\(\\mathcal A\\) only when \\(c\\ge 2k+1\\); indeed, if \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal A\\) lies on the diagonal then \\(b=a+(b-a)\\ge a+k\\), whence \\(c=a+b\\ge 2a+k\\ge 2k+1\\).  \n\n* Moreover, if a diagonal meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) then all such points have the same \\(x\\)-coordinate, i.e. the diagonal intersects **at most one** column of \\(C_A\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A necessary inequality for \\(k\\)\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nv=\\#\\text{vertical lines},\\qquad\nh=\\#\\text{horizontal lines},\\qquad\nd=\\#\\text{diagonal lines}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the total number of lines is \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nv+h+d=n-k. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n**Covering \\(\\mathcal A\\).**  \nEvery column of \\(C_A\\) must contain a line meeting \\(\\mathcal A\\). Only vertical and diagonal lines can do this, and each diagonal can serve at most one column. Hence\n\n\\[\nv+d\\ge |C_A|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n**Covering \\(\\mathcal B\\).**  \nEvery row of \\(R_B\\) must contain a line meeting \\(\\mathcal B\\). Horizontal lines each cover exactly one row; a diagonal line (if present) covers all rows of \\(R_B\\). Therefore\n\n\\[\nh+\\mathbf 1_{\\{d\\ge 1\\}}\\ge |R_B|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAdding (8) and (9) and using (7) we obtain\n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-\n\\mathbf 1_{\\{d\\ge 1\\}} .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\mathbf 1_{\\{d\\ge 1\\}}\\) is either \\(0\\) or \\(1\\), the right\u2011hand side is maximal when it equals \\(0\\). Consequently (10) implies\n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor-1 .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nA short case analysis on the parity of \\(n\\) shows that (11) is equivalent to\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThus (12) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of a suitable family of lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Constructions showing sufficiency\n\nWe now prove that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) there exists a configuration satisfying the requirements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.1  Case \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThe family contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n*Verification.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(y=x+(b-a)\\).\n\n* Otherwise \\(b-a\\ge k\\) or \\(b-a\\le-1\\).  \n  From (4) we have \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\). Because \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\),\n\n  \\[\n  \\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k,\n  \\tag{14}\n  \\]\n\n  (indeed \\(2(n-k)-(n+1-k)=n-1-k\\ge1\\)).  \n  Hence \\(a\\le n-k\\) and the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (which belongs to the set \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\)) contains the point.  \n  The same inequality also yields \\(a\\le n-k\\) when \\(b-a\\le-1\\) (using \\(b\\le\\frac{n}{2}\\le n-k\\)).  \n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.2  Case \\(k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n\nWrite \\(k=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\). Set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-2k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,k\\,\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}} .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n-2k\\ge0\\) (equality when \\(n\\) is even), the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-2k)+k=n .\n\\]\n\n*Verification.*  \nTake \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) the point lies on a sunny line.\n\n* If \\(b-a\\ge k\\) then from \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n  \\[\n  a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}. \\tag{16}\n  \\]\n\n  \u2013 **Even \\(n\\) (\\(n=2k\\)).**  \n    Then (16) gives \\(a\\le k=n-2k\\); the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present because we included all \\(x=1,\\dots ,n-2k\\)) contains the point.\n\n  \u2013 **Odd \\(n\\) (\\(n=2k+1\\)).**  \n    Here (16) yields \\(a\\le k+1\\).  \n    If \\(a=1\\) we are again covered by the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \n    If \\(a\\ge2\\) we use a horizontal line: from \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) and \\(b-a\\ge k\\) we deduce  \n\n    \\[\n    b\\le n+1-a\\le (2k+2)-a\\le k .\n    \\]\n\n    Hence \\(b\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which belongs to the set \\(\\{y=1,\\dots ,k\\}\\)) contains the point.\n\n* If \\(b-a\\le-1\\) then \\(b\\le\\frac{n}{2}=k\\); consequently the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (present in the construction) contains the point.\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered in all sub\u2011cases.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nInequality (12) shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nConversely, the explicit families (13) for \\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\) and (15) for \\(k=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) provide, for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
