\\mathcal A\\) and \\(\\mathcal B\\).**  \n\n   *Vertical* line \\(x=c\\): meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) iff \\(c\\in C_A\\); it meets **every** point of \\(\\mathcal B\\) (because \\(\\mathcal B\\) contains points with any admissible \\(x\\)).  \n\n   *Horizontal* line \\(y=c\\): meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) iff \\(c\\in R_B\\); it never meets \\(\\mathcal A\\).  \n\n   *Diagonal* line \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)):  \n\n   \u2013 It meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) for every integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) (the point \\((1,c-1)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal B\\)).  \n\n   \u2013 It meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) only when \\(c\\ge 2k+1\\); in that case it can intersect **at most one** column of \\(C_A\\) (the column is forced by the equation \\(a=c-b\\)).  \n\n   \u2013 Consequently a diagonal line can cover **at most one** row of \\(R_B\\) and **at most one** column of \\(C_A\\).\n\n6. **Deriving a necessary bound on \\(k\\).**  \n\n   Suppose a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   v=\\#\\text{vertical lines},\\qquad\n   h=\\#\\text{horizontal lines},\\qquad\n   d=\\#\\text{diagonal lines}.\n   \\]\n\n   Because the total number of lines is \\(n\\),\n\n   \\[\n   v+h+d=n-k. \\tag{6.1}\n   \\]\n\n   *Covering the columns of \\(C_A\\).*  \n   Every column of \\(C_A\\) must contain a line that meets \\(\\mathcal A\\). Only vertical and diagonal lines can do this, and each diagonal can serve **at most one** column. Hence  \n\n   \\[\n   v+d\\ge |C_A|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{6.2}\n   \\]\n\n   *Covering the rows of \\(R_B\\).*  \n   Every row of \\(R_B\\) must contain a line that meets \\(\\mathcal B\\). Horizontal lines each cover exactly one row; a diagonal line can cover **at most one** row (the point where it meets that row). Therefore  \n\n   \\[\n   h+d\\ge |R_B|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{6.3}\n   \\]\n\n   Adding (6.2) and (6.3) and using (6.1) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   n-k\\ge\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{6.4}\n   \\]\n\n   A straightforward case analysis on the parity of \\(n\\) and of \\(k\\) shows that (6.4) is equivalent to  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\,k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}. \\tag{6.5}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus (6.5) is a **necessary** condition for the existence of a suitable configuration.\n\n7. **Sufficient constructions.**  \n   For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) we exhibit an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n   *Case\u202fA* \u2013 \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} .\n   \\tag{7.1}\n   \\]\n\n   The family has \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  \n   For any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) either \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) (covered by a sunny line) or \\(b-a\\ge k\\) or \\(b-a\\le-1\\). In the latter situation (3.3) gives \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k\\); hence the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present because \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\)) contains the point. Thus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered.\n\n   *Case\u202fB* \u2013 \\(k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) (the maximal admissible value).  \n   Write \\(k=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\). Define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_k=\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-2k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}}\n   \\;\\cup\\;\n   \\underbrace{\\{\\,y=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,k\\,\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}} .\n   \\tag{7.2}\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(n-2k\\ge0\\) (equality when \\(n\\) is even), the total number of lines is \\(k+(n-2k)+k=n\\).  \n   For a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\) the same case distinction as above works:\n\n   * If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) it lies on a sunny line.  \n   * If \\(b-a\\ge k\\) then \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\).  \n     \u2013 When \\(n\\) is even (\\(n=2k\\)) this gives \\(a\\le k=n-2k\\), so the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present) covers the point.  \n     \u2013 When \\(n\\) is odd (\\(n=2k+1\\)) we have \\(a\\le k+1\\). If \\(a=1\\) we use the vertical line \\(x=1\\); otherwise \\(a\\ge2\\) and from \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) together with \\(b-a\\ge k\\) we obtain \\(b\\le k\\), so the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (present) covers the point.  \n   * If \\(b-a\\le-1\\) then \\(b\\le k\\), and again the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) covers the point.\n\n   Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered for the maximal \\(k\\) as well.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (6.5) shows that \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) is necessary; the explicit families (7.1) and (7.2) prove that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) is attainable. Therefore the admissible values of \\(k\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n   \\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nThus a non\u2011sunny line belongs to exactly one of the families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf H:\\;y=c,\\qquad\n\\mathbf V:\\;x=c,\\qquad\n\\mathbf D:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Choice of sunny lines\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nTake the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\;y=x+t\\qquad(t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\),\n\n\\[\n(a,b)\\in S_t\\iff b-a=t\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDefine the two subsets of points not covered by the sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\ge k\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal B:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\le-1\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Geometry of \\(\\mathcal A\\) and \\(\\mathcal B\\)\n\nFrom \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n(a,b)\\in\\mathcal A &\\Longrightarrow a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2},\\\\\n(a,b)\\in\\mathcal B &\\Longrightarrow b\\le\\frac{n}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nHence all points of \\(\\mathcal A\\) lie in the set of columns  \n\n\\[\nC_A:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}, \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nand all points of \\(\\mathcal B\\) lie in the set of rows  \n\n\\[\nR_B:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Interaction with non\u2011sunny lines\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\): meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) iff \\(c\\in C_A\\); it meets **every** point of \\(\\mathcal B\\) (because \\(\\mathcal B\\) contains points with any admissible \\(x\\)).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\): meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) iff \\(c\\in R_B\\); it never meets \\(\\mathcal A\\).\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)):  \n\n- For any integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the point \\((1,c-1)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal B\\); thus the diagonal meets \\(\\mathcal B\\).  \n- It meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) only when \\(c\\ge 2k+1\\); in that case the equation \\(a=c-b\\) forces a unique column, so the diagonal can intersect **at most one** column of \\(C_A\\).  \n- Similarly, a diagonal can intersect **at most one** row of \\(R_B\\) (the row is determined by the \\(y\\)-coordinate of the intersection point).\n\nConsequently each diagonal line can contribute at most one column of \\(C_A\\) and at most one row of \\(R_B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Necessary condition on \\(k\\)\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering requirement exists and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nv=\\#\\text{vertical lines},\\qquad\nh=\\#\\text{horizontal lines},\\qquad\nd=\\#\\text{diagonal lines}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the total number of lines is \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nv+h+d=n-k. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n**Covering the columns of \\(C_A\\).**  \nEvery column of \\(C_A\\) must contain a line that meets \\(\\mathcal A\\). Only vertical and diagonal lines can do this, and each diagonal can serve at most one column. Hence\n\n\\[\nv+d\\ge |C_A|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n**Covering the rows of \\(R_B\\).**  \nEvery row of \\(R_B\\) must contain a line that meets \\(\\mathcal B\\). Horizontal lines each cover exactly one row; a diagonal can cover at most one row. Therefore\n\n\\[\nh+d\\ge |R_B|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nAdding (8) and (9) and using (7) gives\n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nNow we solve (10) for \\(k\\). Write \\(n=2q\\) or \\(n=2q+1\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2q\\) (even):*  \n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor =q,\\qquad\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n\\begin{cases}\nq-\\frac{k}{2}, & k\\text{ even},\\\\[2pt]\nq-\\frac{k+1}{2}, & k\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nIn both sub\u2011cases inequality (10) becomes \\(2q-k\\ge q+q-\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\), i.e. \\(k\\le q\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2q+1\\) (odd):*  \n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor =q,\\qquad\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n\\begin{cases}\nq+1-\\frac{k}{2}, & k\\text{ even},\\\\[2pt]\nq+1-\\frac{k+1}{2}, & k\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\nInequality (10) simplifies to \\(2q+1-k\\ge q+q+1-\\lceil k/2\\rceil\\), again yielding \\(k\\le q\\).\n\nSince \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor =q\\) in both parity cases, we have proved\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}. \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus (11) is a **necessary** condition.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Sufficient constructions\n\nWe now show that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) can actually be realized.\n\n#### 5.1  The case \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nThe family contains \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n*Verification.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) the point lies on the sunny line \\(y=x+(b-a)\\).  \n* Otherwise \\(b-a\\ge k\\) or \\(b-a\\le-1\\). From (4) we have \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\). Because \\(k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k,\n\\]\n\nso \\(a\\le n-k\\) and the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in the set \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\)) contains the point. Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered.\n\n#### 5.2  The maximal case \\(k=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\n\nWrite \\(k=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\). Set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-2k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,k\\,\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}} .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n-2k\\ge0\\) (equality when \\(n\\) is even), the total number of lines is \\(k+(n-2k)+k=n\\).\n\n*Verification.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n* If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) the point lies on a sunny line.  \n* If \\(b-a\\ge k\\) then \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\).  \n\n  \u2013 When \\(n\\) is even (\\(n=2k\\)) this gives \\(a\\le k=n-2k\\); the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present) covers the point.  \n\n  \u2013 When \\(n\\) is odd (\\(n=2k+1\\)) we have \\(a\\le k+1\\). If \\(a=1\\) we use the vertical line \\(x=1\\); otherwise \\(a\\ge2\\) and from \\(b\\le n+1-a\\) together with \\(b-a\\ge k\\) we obtain \\(b\\le k\\), so the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (present) covers the point.  \n\n* If \\(b-a\\le-1\\) then \\(b\\le k\\); the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) covers the point.\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered for the maximal \\(k\\) as well.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nInequality (11) shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\).  \nThe explicit families (12) for \\(k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\) and (13) for \\(k=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) demonstrate that every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nis attainable. Consequently the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\; } .\n\\]"
