            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff it is not parallel to any of the three directions  \n\n\\[\n\\text{horizontal }(y=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{vertical }(x=\\text{const}),\\qquad\n\\text{slope }-1\\;(x+y=\\text{const}).\n\\]\n\nHence a non\u2011sunny line belongs to exactly one of the families  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbf H:\\;y=c,\\qquad\n\\mathbf V:\\;x=c,\\qquad\n\\mathbf D:\\;x+y=c .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Sunny lines and the uncovered sets\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nTake the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nS_t:\\;y=x+t\\qquad(t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1). \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\),\n\n\\[\n(a,b)\\in S_t\\iff b-a=t\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nDefine the two subsets of points not covered by the sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal A:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\ge k\\},\\qquad\n\\mathcal B:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\mid b-a\\le-1\\}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Bounds on the coordinates\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal A\\). Then \\(b-a\\ge k\\), i.e. \\(b\\ge a+k\\).  \nUsing \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na+(a+k)\\le a+b\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;2a\\le n+1-k,\n\\]\nhence  \n\n\\[\na\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}. \\tag{4a}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, for \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal B\\) we have \\(b-a\\le-1\\), i.e. \\(b\\le a-1\\).  \nAgain \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n(a-1)+a\\le a+b\\le n+1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;2b\\le n-1,\n\\]\nso  \n\n\\[\nb\\le\\frac{n}{2}. \\tag{4b}\n\\]\n\nThus (4a) and (4b) are the precise bounds claimed earlier.\n\nConsequently the possible columns of points of \\(\\mathcal A\\) are  \n\n\\[\nC_A:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nand the possible rows of points of \\(\\mathcal B\\) are  \n\n\\[\nR_B:=\\Bigl\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Interaction of non\u2011sunny lines with \\(\\mathcal A,\\mathcal B\\)\n\n*Vertical line* \\(x=c\\).  \nIf \\(c\\in C_A\\) then the line contains all points of \\(\\mathcal A\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(c\\); otherwise it contains none of \\(\\mathcal A\\).  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal B\\) has some first coordinate \\(a\\ge1\\); the vertical line \\(x=a\\) passes through it, so a vertical line meets **every** point of \\(\\mathcal B\\).\n\n*Horizontal line* \\(y=c\\).  \nIf \\(c\\in R_B\\) the line contains all points of \\(\\mathcal B\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(c\\); otherwise it meets none of \\(\\mathcal B\\).  \nBecause points of \\(\\mathcal A\\) satisfy \\(b\\ge a+k\\ge k\\ge1\\), their second coordinate never belongs to \\(R_B\\) when \\(k\\ge1\\); thus a horizontal line never meets \\(\\mathcal A\\).\n\n*Diagonal line* \\(x+y=c\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \n\n- **Intersection with \\(\\mathcal B\\).**  \n  For any integer \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\) the point \\((1,c-1)\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal B\\) (since \\(c-1\\le n\\) and \\(c-1-(1)\\le -1\\)). Hence every diagonal line with such a constant meets \\(\\mathcal B\\).\n\n- **Intersection with \\(\\mathcal A\\).**  \n  Suppose \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal A\\) lies on the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\). Then \\(b=c-a\\) and, because \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal A\\), we have \\(b-a\\ge k\\). Substituting \\(b\\) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  (c-a)-a\\ge k\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;c\\ge 2a+k.\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n\n  Since \\(a\\) is a positive integer, (7) implies \\(c\\ge 2k+1\\). Conversely, if \\(c\\ge 2k+1\\) and we choose a specific column \\(a\\) with  \n\n  \\[\n  a=\\bigl\\lceil\\frac{c-k}{2}\\bigr\\rceil,\n  \\]\n\n  then \\(b=c-a\\) satisfies \\(b-a\\ge k\\) and \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal A\\) (provided also \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), which holds because \\(c\\le n+1\\)). Thus **a diagonal line can intersect \\(\\mathcal A\\) only when \\(c\\ge 2k+1\\), and for a given \\(c\\) the equation \\(a=c-b\\) determines a unique column \\(a\\).** Hence a diagonal line can meet **at most one** column of \\(C_A\\).\n\n- **Intersection with \\(\\mathcal B\\).**  \n  If a diagonal line meets \\(\\mathcal B\\) at \\((a,b)\\) then \\(b\\le a-1\\). Using \\(b=c-a\\) we obtain  \n\n  \\[\n  c-a\\le a-1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;c\\le 2a-1.\n  \\]\n\n  Since \\(a\\) is fixed, this inequality determines at most one possible value of \\(a\\); consequently the diagonal meets **at most one** row of \\(R_B\\).\n\nThese observations justify the \u201cat most one\u2019\u2019 statements used later.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  A necessary inequality for \\(k\\)\n\nAssume a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition exists and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLet  \n\n\\[\nv=\\#\\text{vertical lines},\\qquad\nh=\\#\\text{horizontal lines},\\qquad\nd=\\#\\text{diagonal lines}.\n\\]\n\nBecause the total number of lines is \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nv+h+d=n-k. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n**Covering the columns of \\(C_A\\).**  \nEvery column \\(c\\in C_A\\) must contain a line that meets \\(\\mathcal A\\). Only vertical lines (which each cover exactly one column) and diagonal lines (which, as proved above, can cover at most one column) are capable of this. Hence\n\n\\[\nv+d\\ge |C_A|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n**Covering the rows of \\(R_B\\).**  \nEvery row \\(r\\in R_B\\) must contain a line that meets \\(\\mathcal B\\). Horizontal lines each cover exactly one row, and a diagonal line can cover at most one row, so\n\n\\[\nh+d\\ge |R_B|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nAdding (9) and (10) and using (8) gives\n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor+\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nWe now solve (11) for \\(k\\). Write \\(n=2q\\) or \\(n=2q+1\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2q\\) (even).*  \nThen \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor =q\\). Moreover\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n\\begin{cases}\nq-\\dfrac{k}{2}, & k\\text{ even},\\\\[4pt]\nq-\\dfrac{k+1}{2}, & k\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nIn both sub\u2011cases (11) becomes  \n\n\\[\n2q-k\\ge q+q-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\n\\]\nwhich simplifies to \\(k\\le q\\).\n\n*If \\(n=2q+1\\) (odd).*  \nThen \\(\\bigl\\lfloor n/2\\bigr\\rfloor =q\\) and\n\n\\[\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n\\begin{cases}\nq+1-\\dfrac{k}{2}, & k\\text{ even},\\\\[4pt]\nq+1-\\dfrac{k+1}{2}, & k\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting into (11) yields  \n\n\\[\n2q+1-k\\ge q+q+1-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{k}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil,\n\\]\nagain equivalent to \\(k\\le q\\).\n\nSince \\(\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor =q\\) in both parity cases, we have proved the **necessary condition**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\,}. \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Constructions showing sufficiency\n\nWe now exhibit, for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and covering all points of \\(\\mathcal P\\).\n\n#### 5.1  Small values of \\(k\\)  \n\nAssume  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}} .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe family has \\(k+(n-k)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.\n\n*Verification.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)*, the point lies on the sunny line \\(y=x+(b-a)\\).\n\n*Otherwise* we have either \\(b-a\\ge k\\) or \\(b-a\\le-1\\).  \nFrom (4a) we know that in the first case \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\).  \nBecause of (13),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k,\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nwhich is proved by elementary algebra:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\le n-k\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nn+1-k\\le 2n-2k\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nk\\le n-1,\n\\]\n\nand \\(k\\le\\frac{n-2}{2}\\) certainly implies \\(k\\le n-1\\).  \nThus \\(a\\le n-k\\), so the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present in the set \\(\\{x=1,\\dots ,n-k\\}\\)) contains the point.  \n\nIf \\(b-a\\le-1\\) then \\(b\\le a-1\\le n-k-1\\le n-k\\); the same vertical line \\(x=a\\) again covers the point.  \nHence every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered.\n\n#### 5.2  The maximal value of \\(k\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nk=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=x+t\\mid t=0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\,\\}}_{\\text{sunny}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,x=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,n-2k\\,\\}}_{\\text{vertical}}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\underbrace{\\{\\,y=c\\mid c=1,2,\\dots ,k\\,\\}}_{\\text{horizontal}} .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(n-2k\\ge0\\) (equality when \\(n\\) is even), the total number of lines is  \n\n\\[\nk+(n-2k)+k=n,\n\\]\n\nand exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n*Verification.*  \nTake any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\)*, the point lies on a sunny line.\n\n*Otherwise* we have \\(b-a\\ge k\\) or \\(b-a\\le-1\\).\n\n- **Case \\(b-a\\ge k\\).**  \n  From (4a) we have \\(a\\le\\frac{n+1-k}{2}\\).\n\n  *If \\(n\\) is even*, then \\(n=2k\\) and \\(\\frac{n+1-k}{2}=k\\).  \n  Hence \\(a\\le k=n-2k\\), so the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (present because the vertical set runs up to \\(n-2k\\)) covers the point.\n\n  *If \\(n\\) is odd*, write \\(n=2k+1\\). Then  \n\n  \\[\n  a\\le\\frac{2k+2-k}{2}=k+1.\n  \\tag{18}\n  \\]\n\n  If \\(a=1\\) we use the vertical line \\(x=1\\) (which belongs to the vertical set because \\(n-2k=1\\)).  \n  If \\(a\\ge2\\), combine \\(a+b\\le n+1=2k+2\\) with \\(b-a\\ge k\\) to obtain  \n\n  \\[\n  b\\le 2k+2-a\\le 2k+2-2=k,\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. \\(b\\le k\\). The horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (present because the horizontal set contains all integers \\(1,\\dots ,k\\)) therefore contains the point.\n\n- **Case \\(b-a\\le-1\\).**  \n  Then \\(b\\le a-1\\le k\\) because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(b\\le\\frac{n}{2}=k\\) by (4b). Hence the horizontal line \\(y=b\\) (which exists) covers the point.\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P\\) is covered for the maximal \\(k\\) as well.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nThe necessary inequality (12) shows that any feasible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nConversely, the explicit families (14) for \\(0\\le k\\le\\lfloor\\frac{n-2}{2}\\rfloor\\) and (17) for \\(k=\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) provide constructions for **every** integer \\(k\\) in the range  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nTherefore the set of all admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
