ome first coordinate \\(a\\ge1\\); the vertical line \\(x=a\\) passes through it, so a vertical line meets **every** point of \\(\\mathcal B\\).\u201d  \n  **Analysis:** A vertical line \\(x=c\\) can only meet points whose first coordinate equals \\(c\\); it cannot meet all points of \\(\\mathcal B\\). The statement is therefore false. The later argument does not rely on this claim, but the justification is missing.  \n  **Classification:** **Justification Gap** (incorrect statement, but not fatal to the proof).\n\n#### Horizontal lines\n- The observation that a horizontal line never meets \\(\\mathcal A\\) for \\(k\\ge1\\) follows from the bounds on \\(b\\) and is correct.\n\n#### Diagonal lines\n- The discussion leading to inequalities (7) and the conclusion that a diagonal can intersect at most one column of \\(C_A\\) and at most one row of \\(R_B\\) is plausible, but the argument is only sketched. A rigorous proof would need to show uniqueness of the integer solution for a given constant \\(c\\).  \n  **Classification:** **Justification Gap** \u2013 the \u201cat most one\u2019\u2019 statements are used later without a full proof.\n\n### 4. A necessary inequality for \\(k\\)\n\n- The counting equations (8)\u2013(11) are derived under the assumption that only vertical and diagonal lines can cover columns of \\(\\mathcal A\\), and only horizontal and diagonal lines can cover rows of \\(\\mathcal B\\). This relies on the specific structure of \\(\\mathcal A,\\mathcal B\\) defined in step\u202f1, which itself rests on the unjustified choice of sunny lines of slope\u202f1. Consequently, the inequality (11) and the resulting bound \\(k\\le\\lfloor n/2\\rfloor\\) are **not proven for an arbitrary configuration** of sunny lines.  \n  **Classification:** This is a **consequence of the Critical Error** identified earlier.\n\n### 5. Constructions showing sufficiency\n\n- The constructions (14) and (17) explicitly exhibit families of lines with the required numbers of sunny 